  <p slot="body" class="page-summary" style="position:absolute;left:-9999px;top:auto;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;" aria-hidden="false">The Ultimate Guide to Camping in Dinosaur Country is a practical trip-planning guide to camping near Vernal, Utah in northeastern Utah. It helps visitors decide where to camp, which style of camping fits their trip, and which destination best matches their goals. It covers camping across Flaming Gorge, Ashley National Forest and the Uinta Mountains, Red Fleet State Park, Steinaker State Park, the Dinosaur National Monument area, and Vernal as a basecamp. It compares RV, tent, and dispersed camping; state park, national forest, and monument-area campgrounds; family camping, photography, wildlife safety, accessibility, and seasonal timing; and one-night, weekend, and week-long itineraries. Campground availability, reservation systems, campsite numbers, fees, hookups, dump stations, seasonal opening dates, fire restrictions, and permit requirements change and are set by the U.S. Forest Service, the National Park Service, and Utah State Parks — verify with official sources. Adventure Tours Vernal offers guided UTV tours for $349 per machine, up to 2 riders, 3 hours. Call (435) 219-9447.</p>

  <main class="article-page" slot="body">
    <header class="article-header">
      <h1 class="article-title">The Ultimate Guide to Camping in Dinosaur Country</h1>
      <p class="article-meta">Updated July 2026 · Adventure Tours Vernal</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">

      <div class="article-summary-box"><strong>Quick Answer:</strong> Dinosaur Country — the corner of northeastern Utah around Vernal — is one of the best and least-crowded places to camp in the West, and the trick is matching the destination to the trip you want. Want water, boating, and red-canyon scenery? Camp at <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-flaming-gorge/">Flaming Gorge</a>, about an hour north. Want cool mountain forest and alpine lakes? Head up into <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-ashley-national-forest/">Ashley National Forest</a> and the Uintas. Want easy, family-friendly camping minutes from town? <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-red-fleet-state-park/">Red Fleet</a> and <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-steinaker-state-park/">Steinaker</a> State Parks are made for it. Want to sleep near the fossils and river canyons? Camp in the <a href="/dinosaur-national-monument/visiting-dinosaur-national-monument/">Dinosaur National Monument</a> area. Prefer showers and restaurants close by? Base in <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-vernal-utah/">Vernal</a> and day-trip out. This guide helps you choose your destination <em>and</em> your style — RV, tent, or dispersed — then points you to a dedicated deep-dive for each place. Reserve early for summer, and always verify current fees, openings, and fire rules before you go.</div>

      <div class="key-takeaways">
        <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Five great destinations, one supply town.</strong> Flaming Gorge, Ashley National Forest, Red Fleet, Steinaker, and the Dinosaur National Monument area are all within day-trip range of Vernal, and each offers a genuinely different kind of camping.</li>
          <li><strong>Choose the destination by your goal.</strong> Water and boating, high mountain forest, easy family weekends, or fossils and canyons — each has a best home here, and this guide is built to help you pick.</li>
          <li><strong>Match the style to the trip.</strong> RV, tent, and dispersed camping each suit different travelers and comfort levels; you can even mix them across a longer trip.</li>
          <li><strong>Easy for beginners, wild for the experienced.</strong> You can pull an RV into a developed state-park loop minutes from town, or find a quiet dispersed site under the pines with no one around for miles.</li>
          <li><strong>Always verify the details.</strong> Campground availability, reservations, fees, hookups, seasonal openings, fire restrictions, and permits are set by the managing agencies and change — confirm current specifics before every trip.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>Why Camp in Dinosaur Country</h2>
      <p>Most people know this region for dinosaurs and red rock, not for camping — which is exactly why the camping is so good. While campers pile into the famous Western parks and book out months ahead, Dinosaur Country quietly offers a range of camping that would be a headline destination anywhere else: a 91-mile reservoir ringed with campgrounds, an entire national forest of pine and aspen with hundreds of lakes, two easy lakeside state parks minutes from town, and a national monument of fossils and river canyons — all within an hour or so of a single supply town.</p>
      <p>The real advantage here is <em>variety within a small radius</em>. From one region you can spend a night on a red-rock reservoir shoreline, the next in a cool spruce forest beside an alpine lake, and the next steps from a swimming beach with the kids — without ever driving far. Few places pack big-water camping, high-country forest, easy family reservoirs, and fossil-country canyons into so tight a footprint.</p>
      <p>And it does it without the crowds. This is working, uncrowded country, where you can still find a quiet campsite or a whole stretch of forest to yourself, especially midweek and in the shoulder seasons. For the full picture of the region and how to plan a trip around it, start with our <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-vernal-utah/">Ultimate Guide to Vernal</a>; for everything else there is to do between nights in camp, see <a href="/things-to-do/">things to do in Vernal</a>, <a href="/fishing/">fishing in Dinosaur Country</a>, and the region's <a href="/scenic-drives/">scenic drives</a>.</p>

      <h2>Choosing the Right Camping Destination</h2>
      <p>Think of Dinosaur Country camping as five distinct destinations, each with its own personality. You do not have to choose just one — many trips combine two or three — but knowing what each offers is the key to a good plan.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Destination</th><th>Setting</th><th>Known For</th><th>Distance from Vernal</th><th>Best For</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Flaming Gorge</td><td>Reservoir &amp; red canyon</td><td>Boating, fishing, big-water views, developed campgrounds</td><td>~1 hour north</td><td>Water lovers, RVers, anglers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ashley National Forest / Uintas</td><td>Mountain forest &amp; alpine lakes</td><td>Cool pine forest, hundreds of lakes, solitude, dispersed camping</td><td>~30–90 min north</td><td>Tent campers, dispersed campers, summer heat-escapers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Red Fleet State Park</td><td>Small reservoir, red sandstone</td><td>Swimming, paddling, a dinosaur trackway, easy access</td><td>~15 min north</td><td>Families, first-timers, short trips</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Steinaker State Park</td><td>Small reservoir near town</td><td>Swimming, fishing, the closest camping to Vernal</td><td>~5–10 min north</td><td>Families, spontaneous overnights</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dinosaur National Monument area</td><td>Fossil canyons &amp; rivers</td><td>Quarry fossils, petroglyphs, Green &amp; Yampa river canyons</td><td>~20 min east (Quarry area)</td><td>History &amp; geology travelers, river-canyon campers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Vernal (basecamp)</td><td>Town</td><td>Hotels, private RV parks, restaurants, showers, supplies</td><td>—</td><td>Comfort seekers, RVers wanting hookups, rainy-week backups</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p><em>Distances are approximate and depend on your exact destination within each area. Campground availability, amenities, and access change over time. <strong>VERIFY WITH OFFICIAL SOURCE</strong> (the Forest Service, National Park Service, or Utah State Parks) for current campgrounds, sites, and conditions at each destination.</em></p>
      <p>With so many good options, the real question is which one fits <em>your</em> trip. Use this quick decision guide to point yourself at the right destination, then read that place's dedicated cornerstone guide before you go.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>If you want…</th><th>Camp at…</th><th>Why</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Water, boating, and big scenery</td><td>Flaming Gorge</td><td>A 91-mile reservoir with developed campgrounds and marinas</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cool forest and mountain lakes</td><td>Ashley National Forest / Uintas</td><td>High-country pine, aspen, and hundreds of lakes; escapes the heat</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Solitude and free, primitive camping</td><td>Ashley National Forest (dispersed)</td><td>Vast public land where dispersed camping is generally allowed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An easy family weekend close to town</td><td>Red Fleet or Steinaker</td><td>Developed lakeside sites, swimming, and short drives</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Fossils, petroglyphs, and river canyons</td><td>Dinosaur National Monument area</td><td>Camp near the Quarry, the rivers, and the geology</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Showers, restaurants, and full hookups</td><td>Vernal (basecamp)</td><td>Town comforts with every destination a day trip away</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Camping Styles: Which One Fits Your Trip</h2>
      <p>Before you pick a campground, it helps to decide <em>how</em> you want to camp. The three main styles — RV, tent, and dispersed — suit very different travelers, and each destination above supports some better than others.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Style</th><th>Comfort &amp; amenities</th><th>Best for</th><th>Where it shines here</th><th>Watch-outs</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>RV / trailer</td><td>Highest — bring your beds, kitchen, and climate control</td><td>Families, longer stays, comfort seekers, shoulder seasons</td><td>Flaming Gorge, state parks, private Vernal RV parks</td><td>Site length limits, hookup availability, narrow forest roads</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tent (developed)</td><td>Moderate — a designated site with facilities nearby</td><td>Most campers, families new to camping, mountain trips</td><td>All developed campgrounds; forest campgrounds for cool nights</td><td>Weather exposure, summer weekends fill up</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dispersed / primitive</td><td>Lowest — no facilities, you pack in and out</td><td>Experienced, self-sufficient campers wanting solitude</td><td>Ashley National Forest backcountry and forest roads</td><td>No water, toilets, or trash; strict rules; fire bans; road/weather risk</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>You can also mix styles on a longer trip — a developed RV site at Flaming Gorge to start, a couple of nights tent-camping in the forest, and a dispersed night under the stars to finish. The sections below go deeper on each.</p>

      <h2>RV Camping in Dinosaur Country</h2>
      <p>Dinosaur Country is friendly to RVers, and the region gives you a full range from full-service town parks to rustic forest loops. Developed campgrounds at <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-flaming-gorge/">Flaming Gorge</a> and the state parks generally include RV-suitable sites, private RV parks in and around <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-vernal-utah/">Vernal</a> add full-hookup convenience, and parts of <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-ashley-national-forest/">Ashley National Forest</a> have campgrounds that accommodate smaller rigs and trailers.</p>
      <p>The key variables for RV travelers are the ones that change most and that we will never invent for you: whether a given campground offers <strong>hookups</strong> (electric, water, sewer), whether it has a <strong>dump station</strong> and potable water, its <strong>maximum site length</strong>, and whether it has <strong>pull-through</strong> sites. These vary by campground and by season, and the highway approaches to some high-country campgrounds are narrow and winding. Before you tow anything up a forest road, confirm the site size and access.</p>
      <div class="article-tip"><strong>RV reality check:</strong> the biggest, best-serviced RV sites are at Flaming Gorge, the state parks, and private Vernal parks; the forest is more rustic and better suited to smaller rigs. For any specific campground's hookups, dump station, length limits, and pull-through availability, <strong>VERIFY WITH OFFICIAL SOURCE</strong> (the managing agency or park) — and reserve early for summer, when the best RV sites fill first.</div>

      <h2>Tent Camping in Dinosaur Country</h2>
      <p>For tent campers, this region is a gift: you can pitch beside a red-rock reservoir, under tall pines by a mountain lake, or on a warm swimming-beach loop with the kids, all within an hour of one another. Developed tent camping is available across the destinations, and the experience changes dramatically with elevation — warm high-desert nights near the reservoirs and town, and genuinely cold nights up in the <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-ashley-national-forest/">Uinta forest</a> even in midsummer.</p>
      <p>That elevation range is the thing to plan around. Summer nights near Vernal, Red Fleet, and Steinaker are mild; the same night at a high forest campground can drop toward freezing. Bring a warmer sleeping bag than the daytime heat suggests, stake well against afternoon wind and thunderstorms, and choose your elevation to suit the forecast. For a heat wave, climb into the forest; for a cold snap, drop back toward town.</p>
      <p>Tent camping is the most flexible, lowest-cost way to sample several destinations on one trip, and it is the classic way to experience the mountains. Pack for the environment — see our <a href="/guides/what-to-bring/">what to bring guide</a> — and time it with the <a href="/guides/vernal-weather-guide/">Vernal weather guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>Dispersed Camping Overview</h2>
      <p>Beyond the developed campgrounds lies one of the region's best-kept secrets: <strong>dispersed camping</strong> — primitive, no-facilities camping on public land, generally allowed across much of <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-ashley-national-forest/">Ashley National Forest</a>. For self-sufficient campers, it means solitude, no reservations, and often no fee, in country most visitors never see.</p>
      <p>It also means no water, no toilets, no trash service, and no safety net. Dispersed camping comes with real responsibilities and rules — where you may camp, how far from water and roads, how long you may stay, and what fire restrictions apply — and those rules vary by area and change through the season. This guide is a planning overview, not a rulebook: a dedicated dispersed-camping spoke is planned to cover the specifics.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Know before you go.</strong> Dispersed-camping areas, stay limits, and closures are set by the agency and change. <strong>VERIFY WITH OFFICIAL SOURCE</strong> (Ashley National Forest) for current rules before you rely on a spot.</li>
        <li><strong>Be fully self-sufficient.</strong> Carry all your water, a way to handle human waste, and everything you need to pack out every scrap of trash.</li>
        <li><strong>Fire is the big one.</strong> Fire restrictions change with conditions and are often in force; never assume a fire is allowed, and confirm the current rules for that exact area.</li>
        <li><strong>Leave No Trace.</strong> Use existing sites where they exist, camp on durable surfaces, keep well away from water sources, and leave the spot better than you found it.</li>
        <li><strong>Mind the roads and weather.</strong> Forest roads can be rough, muddy when wet, and impassable when snowy; cell service is spotty. Tell someone your plan.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>State Park Camping: Red Fleet &amp; Steinaker</h2>
      <p>For the easiest, most family-friendly camping in the region, the two state parks minutes north of town are the answer. Both combine developed campgrounds with swimming, fishing, and paddling right at camp, and both are short, forgiving drives from Vernal's supplies and services.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-red-fleet-state-park/">Red Fleet State Park</a> sits among dramatic red sandstone and is best known for its reservoir and its famous <strong>dinosaur trackway</strong> — a genuine adventure you can reach by trail or paddle. Camping here pairs a lakeside night with swimming, shore fishing, and the trackway, which makes it a standout for families who want nature and a real point of interest without a long drive. Camping at Red Fleet State Park is one of the easiest family camping experiences in Dinosaur Country — read our <a href="/camping/camping-at-red-fleet-state-park/">complete guide to camping at Red Fleet</a>.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-steinaker-state-park/">Steinaker State Park</a> is the closest camping to Vernal — just five to ten minutes north — which makes it the go-to for a spontaneous overnight or a first family camping trip. Its reservoir is a summer swimming, paddling, and fishing spot, and the short distance to town means you are never far from a forgotten supply.</p>
      <div class="article-tip"><strong>Plan the parks properly:</strong> the two dedicated guides — <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-red-fleet-state-park/">Red Fleet</a> and <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-steinaker-state-park/">Steinaker</a> — cover access, recreation, and camping in full. Utah state park campgrounds commonly take reservations and fill on summer weekends. For current campsites, fees, hookups, and reservation windows, <strong>VERIFY WITH OFFICIAL SOURCE</strong> (Utah State Parks) and book early.</div>

      <h2>National Forest Camping: Ashley &amp; the Uintas</h2>
      <p>North and west of Vernal, the land climbs into the Uinta Mountains and the <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-ashley-national-forest/">Ashley National Forest</a> — and with it, the region's most expansive camping. This is cool, high-country camping among pine and aspen, near hundreds of lakes and streams, and it is where locals escape the summer heat.</p>
      <p>The forest offers two very different experiences. <strong>Developed forest campgrounds</strong> give you a designated site, typically with basic facilities, in beautiful settings near lakes and drainages — think drive-up mountain camping with a picnic table and a fire ring where permitted. <strong>Dispersed camping</strong> across much of the forest offers solitude and primitive sites for the self-sufficient (see the section above). Between them, the forest suits everyone from a family in a small trailer to a backpacker heading into the High Uintas Wilderness.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is season and access. The high country has a <strong>short season</strong>, can hold snow into early summer and see it again in early fall, and is reached by roads that range from paved highway to rough dirt. Nights are cold, afternoon thunderstorms are common, services are far, and cell coverage is thin. That is the price of the best scenery and the coolest air in the region.</p>
      <p>Looking for mountain camping? Our complete guide to <a href="/camping/camping-in-ashley-national-forest/">camping in Ashley National Forest</a> breaks down the forest's camping areas, developed versus dispersed sites, alpine and family camping, and how to use a campground as a backpacking basecamp.</p>
      <div class="article-tip"><strong>High-country reality:</strong> see the <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-ashley-national-forest/">Ashley National Forest guide</a> for access and planning. Forest campground openings, road status, fees, and fire restrictions change every year and by area — <strong>VERIFY WITH OFFICIAL SOURCE</strong> (Ashley National Forest) for current conditions before you head up.</div>

      <h2>Camping at Flaming Gorge</h2>
      <p>If Dinosaur Country has a camping headliner, it is <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-flaming-gorge/">Flaming Gorge</a> — a 91-mile reservoir straddling the Utah–Wyoming border, ringed with recreation areas, marinas, and campgrounds and set against fiery red canyon walls. It is the region's premier destination for <strong>combining camping with water</strong>: boating, world-class fishing, paddling, swimming, and big-sky scenery, with developed campgrounds that range from RV-friendly to more primitive shoreline sites.</p>
      <p>Camping here puts you steps from the water for a morning on the reservoir — chasing trophy lake trout and browns (see <a href="/fishing/">fishing in Dinosaur Country</a>), launching a boat, or simply watching the light move across the canyon. The area's developed campgrounds and nearby services make it workable for RVers and tent campers alike, and it connects to the region's best <a href="/scenic-drives/">scenic driving</a> along the Flaming Gorge–Uintas Byway.</p>
      <p>Because Flaming Gorge is popular and its recreation areas each have their own campgrounds, sites can fill in peak summer, and amenities differ from one area to the next. Boaters should also note Utah's clean-drain-dry invasive-species rules and possible inspections.</p>
      <p>Planning to stay overnight? Our complete guide to <a href="/camping/camping-at-flaming-gorge/">camping at Flaming Gorge</a> breaks down the reservoir's camping areas, RV versus tent and shoreline sites, and how to fish and boat right from camp.</p>
      <div class="article-tip"><strong>Plan the Gorge:</strong> the <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-flaming-gorge/">Ultimate Guide to Flaming Gorge</a> covers the recreation areas, marinas, ramps, and access in full. Campground availability, fees, hookups, dump stations, and reservation options vary by area and change — <strong>VERIFY WITH OFFICIAL SOURCE</strong> (the Forest Service and the official reservation service) before your trip, and reserve early for summer weekends.</div>

      <h2>Campgrounds Near Dinosaur National Monument</h2>
      <p>To wake up near the fossils and the river canyons, camp in the <a href="/dinosaur-national-monument/visiting-dinosaur-national-monument/">Dinosaur National Monument</a> area. The monument spans the Utah–Colorado border and protects the famous fossil <strong>Quarry</strong>, ancient <strong>petroglyphs</strong>, and the dramatic canyons of the Green and Yampa rivers — and camping here lets you be at the Quarry early and explore the geology and river country at the day's best light.</p>
      <p>The monument area offers campgrounds in striking canyon-and-river settings, and Vernal — about twenty minutes west of the Utah entrance — provides the nearest full services as a basecamp alternative. Camping near the monument pairs naturally with the <a href="/dinosaur-national-monument/petroglyphs-rock-art-vernal/">petroglyphs and rock art</a>, the <a href="/scenic-drives/cub-creek-road-tour-of-the-tilted-rocks/">Cub Creek scenic drive</a>, and the river canyons, making it the base of choice for travelers who came for the history and the geology.</p>
      <div class="article-tip"><strong>Plan the monument:</strong> see <a href="/dinosaur-national-monument/visiting-dinosaur-national-monument/">Visiting Dinosaur National Monument</a> for the Quarry, fees, and access. National Park Service campgrounds vary in season, amenities, and whether they take reservations or are first-come, first-served, and some have seasonal water. The monument also charges an entrance fee. <strong>VERIFY WITH OFFICIAL SOURCE</strong> (the National Park Service) for current campgrounds, fees, and openings before you go.</div>

      <h2>Vernal as a Basecamp</h2>
      <p>Not every great camping trip means sleeping in the dirt. For many visitors — especially families with young kids, travelers hitting a rainy stretch, or anyone who wants a hot shower and a restaurant at the end of the day — the smartest play is to base in <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-vernal-utah/">Vernal</a> and day-trip to the destinations above. The town has hotels, private RV parks, restaurants, fuel, groceries, and outdoor supplies, and every camping destination in this guide is within about an hour's drive.</p>
      <p>Basecamping also makes a fine hybrid: camp out on the good-weather nights, retreat to town when a storm rolls in or the group needs a reset, and keep the trip comfortable without giving up the country. It is the lowest-friction way to sample Flaming Gorge, the forest, the state parks, and the monument on one trip. Coming from out of state? Our <a href="/from/salt-lake-city/">Salt Lake City to Vernal guide</a> covers the drive in, and once you are here, <a href="/things-to-do/">things to do in Vernal</a> fills the days between destinations.</p>

      <h2>Family Camping</h2>
      <p>This may be the best family-camping region in Utah that nobody talks about, for one simple reason: it makes camping <em>easy</em> to succeed at. <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-red-fleet-state-park/">Red Fleet</a> and <a href="/guides/ultimate-guide-to-steinaker-state-park/">Steinaker</a> give families a developed lakeside site, swimming and paddling at camp, forgiving short drives, and town just minutes away for the inevitable forgotten item. Add the dinosaur trackway at Red Fleet and you have combined a camping trip with a real adventure the kids will remember.</p>
      <p>For families ready for a bit more nature, developed campgrounds at Flaming Gorge and drive-up sites in the forest add scenery and space while keeping facilities close. Save dispersed and backcountry camping for when the kids are older and the group is experienced — the lack of water, toilets, and a safety net raises the stakes.</p>
      <p>The formula for a great family camping trip here: pick a developed campground with water at camp, keep the first trip short and close (Steinaker or Red Fleet), let the day flex between swimming, exploring, and camp time, and prepare for both high-desert heat and cold nights. For more kid-focused ideas around town, see <a href="/things-to-do/fun-things-to-do-vernal-utah-kids/">fun things to do in Vernal with kids</a>, and pack with our <a href="/guides/what-to-bring/">what to bring guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>Photography Opportunities</h2>
      <p>A camping trip here is also a photography trip, and camping puts you in the right places at the right hours — dawn and dusk, when the light is best and most day-trippers are gone.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Shoot the golden hours from camp.</strong> Camping means you are already on location for sunrise and sunset — the warm, low light on red canyon walls at Flaming Gorge and Red Fleet is unbeatable.</li>
        <li><strong>Catch the reflections.</strong> Calm morning water at the reservoirs and mountain lakes mirrors red rock and peaks; get up before the wind.</li>
        <li><strong>Chase the dark skies.</strong> Away from town, the high desert and the forest deliver superb star photography and Milky Way opportunities on clear, moonless nights.</li>
        <li><strong>Frame the forest color.</strong> In early fall the aspens of the Uintas turn gold — plan a forest camp around the change.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect your gear.</strong> Dust, afternoon storms, and dew are all in play; a dry bag and a lens cloth earn their keep.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch the storms.</strong> Dramatic skies make great photos but dangerous conditions — get off exposed ridges and open water when lightning threatens.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Wildlife Safety</h2>
      <p>Camping puts you in wildlife country, and a few habits keep both you and the animals safe. The single most important is <strong>food storage</strong>: store all food, trash, and scented items secured — in a hard-sided vehicle or per the campground's posted rules — never in your tent, so you do not attract animals into camp. Clean up thoroughly after every meal and pack out all scraps.</p>
      <p>Beyond food, keep a respectful distance from all wildlife — deer, elk, moose, pronghorn, and smaller animals — and never approach or feed them; a moose in the forest deserves particular space. Watch for animals on the road at dawn and dusk when driving to and from camp. In the high country, be storm- and cold-aware; in the high desert, watch where you put your hands and feet on warm days and shake out shoes and gear.</p>
      <div class="article-tip"><strong>Note:</strong> specific wildlife-safety and food-storage requirements can vary by campground and area and may change with conditions. Follow all posted rules at your campground, and <strong>VERIFY WITH OFFICIAL SOURCE</strong> (the managing agency) for current requirements where you are camping.</div>

